prompt:
A photograph taken over a bench in a hospital laboratory's specimen reception, looking straight down at the rack a technician has just finished loading, with the stock tube standing on the bench beside the rack.

Today's request card for this patient, lying open on the bench, asks for four tests, and the department's portion sizes are the usual ones:

  - liver function, which the analyser takes in two separate portions;
  - urea and electrolytes, which the analyser also takes in two separate portions;
  - thyroid function, one portion;
  - C-reactive protein, one portion.

Every portion the technician draws off is one millilitre. The stock tube came up from phlebotomy holding ten millilitres and is a graduated tube, its scale running from nought to ten with every millilitre numbered up the glass, so how much is left in it can be seen against the graduations. Each small tube in the rack carries a handwritten label saying what it is for.

The technician has finished the whole job and this photograph is the one that goes into the record. How many tubes ended up in the rack, what each of them says, and where the surface in the stock tube ended up are all consequences of doing the job properly, and I am deliberately not telling you any of them.

The photograph is a record, so nothing may be added that does the reader's work: no arrow, no ring drawn round a tube, no sticky note, no marker line on the glass and no wording anywhere except the labels on the tubes and the printing already on the request card.
